  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 248 PM EDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\n===== Pleasant Afternoons with Chilly Mornings and Valley Fog =====\n\nWeather conditions continue to be warm, sunny, and pleasant across \nthe whole area. Temperatures are all in the low 70s with all but \ncalm winds. The center of a ridge axis is parked directly overhead \ncausing the nice weather. Despite the ongoing mixing, temperatures \nwill tumble to the saturation point across multiple areas in the CWA \novernight with lows in the low 40s and upper 30s. In the deepest \ndecoupled valleys overnight, dense fog is expected to form along \nwith patchy frost as isolated spots could reach the low to mid 30s. \nThe frost potential will remain confined to our easternmost valleys. \nFog will form in low lying areas across the CWA, meaning another SPS \nwill be issued for patchy dense fog, though confidence is a little \nlow for a Dense Fog Advisory. Highest fog chances are near 40%, with \nunder 20% chances in areas further west.\n\nDespite the chilly and potentially foggy morning, the CWA will mix \nout quite nicely Thursday afternoon. A very weak cold front will \nmove in from the NW from a departing low pressure in Canada. May not \nsee or feel much of a difference as this passes by. At most, this \nfront will knock our highs down by a degree or two compared to \ntoday. Sunny skies will linger and the threat for more valley fog \nwill persist Thursday night into Friday morning.\n\n===== Stagnant, Slow Pattern Change Until Next Week =====\n\nBy the weekend, the ridging at the surface will be reinforced with \nridging at higher levels, indicating little changes in our weather. \nMeans that highs in the low to mid 70s will continue along with the \ndaily valley fog threat each morning. Mid level ridging slowly \nweakens along with the surface high pressure by early next week. \nLong range trends are painting a picture of an approaching trough \nweakening and lifting north as it tries to push into the antecedent \ndry air mass we'll still have in place by Tuesday-Wednesday. PoPs \nremain under 10% which means rainfall is highly unlikely. We may \nreturn into a wet/busy pattern by Halloween weekend, although \nconfidence this far out is really low.",
